Absolutely. Let's redo Day 2 properly, like a first-year graduate-level lesson, but with concrete examples throughout.

Day 2 — Embeddings, Vectors, Similarity & RAG

Yesterday we learned how an LLM generates text.

Today we learn how AI systems represent information so that machines can work with meaning, not just exact words.

The central chain is:

Text → tokens → representations/vectors → similarity → retrieval → LLM context


---

1. First: What problem are embeddings solving?

Imagine you have these three sentences:

A

> I forgot my password.



B

> I can't remember my login credentials.



C

> I had chicken biryani for lunch.



A human immediately understands:

A ↔ B   very similar
A ↔ C   unrelated

But a simple computer doing string matching sees:

A: forgot password
B: remember login credentials

Almost none of the words match.

So traditional keyword matching can fail.

We want a representation where:

"I forgot my password"

and

"I can't remember my login credentials"

end up close together mathematically.

That's what embeddings are for.


---

2. What exactly is an embedding?

An embedding is a numerical representation of some input.

For example, imagine an embedding model gives:

"I forgot my password"

→ [0.82, -0.11, 0.43, 0.91, -0.20, ...]

Maybe:

"I can't remember my login credentials"

→ [0.79, -0.09, 0.40, 0.88, -0.18, ...]

And:

"I ate chicken biryani"

→ [-0.31, 0.74, 0.02, -0.62, 0.55, ...]

The first two vectors are much more similar.

The third is farther away.

Important: don't interpret an individual dimension as something human-readable like:

dimension 1 = password
dimension 2 = food

The representation is distributed across many dimensions.


---

3. What is a vector?

A vector is just an ordered list of numbers.

For example:

[0.2, 0.5, -0.1, 0.8]

You already know this concept from mathematics.

Think of it as a point in space.

With 2 dimensions:

(3, 4)

is a point on a 2D plane.

With 3 dimensions:

(3, 4, 7)

is a point in 3D space.

An embedding might have hundreds or thousands of dimensions.

So conceptually:

Text
  ↓
Embedding model
  ↓
[0.21, -0.72, 0.45, ...]


---

4. Why do thousands of dimensions help?

Suppose we tried representing a document using only three numbers:

[topic, sentiment, length]

That would be far too simplistic.

A real embedding can capture many interacting properties.

For example, two documents may differ in:

topic
language
style
intent
domain
meaning
entities
relationship between concepts

A high-dimensional representation gives the model much more room to encode useful information.

Think of it as:

3 dimensions
    ↓
too little space

1000+ dimensions
    ↓
much richer representation

But don't make the mistake of thinking:

> "More dimensions automatically means better."



It doesn't. Quality depends on the embedding model, training objective, and use case.


---

5. Token vs embedding

This is one of the most important Day 2 distinctions.

Suppose:

> "I love pizza."



After tokenization, it might become something conceptually like:

["I", " love", " pizza", "."]

or token IDs such as:

[40, 1842, 9281, 13]

Those IDs are not semantic representations.

Now an embedding model might produce:

[0.12, -0.44, 0.81, ...]

So:

TOKEN
↓
symbol / identifier

EMBEDDING
↓
numeric representation useful for semantic comparison

Example

The words:

> car



and

> automobile



have completely different token IDs.

But their embeddings can be quite close.

That's exactly what we want.


---

6. An important subtlety: embeddings inside a Transformer

Here's where terminology gets confusing.

A Transformer itself also converts token IDs into vectors very early in its processing.

Conceptually:

"I love pizza"
      ↓
token IDs
      ↓
token embeddings
      ↓
Transformer
      ↓
contextual representations

These internal vectors are part of how the model computes.

When an AI engineer says:

> "Let's generate embeddings for our documents."



they usually mean using a dedicated embedding model/API to produce vectors for search, retrieval, clustering, recommendations, etc.

So the same word—embedding—can appear in two contexts.


---

7. Example: Search engine

Imagine an online store with:

10 million products

User searches:

> "running shoes for marathon"



A keyword search might look for:

running
shoes
marathon

Now consider a product titled:

> "Lightweight carbon-plated race trainers"



No word "marathon" appears.

Keyword search may miss it.

Semantic search can understand that:

"running shoes for marathon"

and

"carbon-plated race trainers"

are related.

The pipeline:

Product descriptions
      ↓
Embedding model
      ↓
Vectors
      ↓
Vector database

User query
      ↓
Embedding model
      ↓
Query vector
      ↓
Find nearest vectors
      ↓
Relevant products


