27. One critical distinction: embeddings don't "contain the document"

This is a common misconception.

Suppose:

Document:
"Azure Contributor role does not automatically grant all data-plane permissions..."

Its embedding is not a compressed copy of the entire document that you can decode later.

Think of the embedding primarily as a representation useful for similarity/retrieval.

You still keep the original text:

Database

document_id
text
embedding
metadata

Then:

embedding → find document
document → give actual text to LLM

That's a crucial architectural pattern.


---

28. Metadata is extremely important

Suppose your vector database contains:

1 million documents

You don't necessarily want semantic search across all of them.

You may want:

cloud = Azure
environment = production
document_type = RBAC
version = 2026

Then:

vector similarity
+
metadata filtering

Example:

> Search only Azure RBAC documents from 2026.



This can dramatically improve retrieval quality.


---

29. Embeddings and multilingual systems

Embeddings become especially interesting with languages.

For example:

"How do I reset my password?"

"पासवर्ड कैसे रीसेट करूँ?"

A good multilingual embedding model may place these near each other.

Then a user can ask in one language and retrieve documents written in another.

That enables:

cross-language search

This is another reason embeddings are much more powerful than keyword matching.


